00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.110
With that out of the
way, Mia Consalvo.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.545
Actually, a lot of you probably
are already familiar,

00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:54.935
you might have taken
any of her classes.

00:00:54.935 --> 00:01:00.904
She is the visiting lecturer--
visiting professor, visiting

00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:02.154
professor--

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.620
here at GAMBIT.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.110
Are you teaching something
in the spring?

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.020
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.940
Cheating and ethics in games.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.090
Brian can tell you
all about it.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.165
PROFESSOR: You can take this
as the sneak preview--

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:18.135
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:01:18.135 --> 00:01:21.530
This is a taster of a little
bit of what we do.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.955
Thanks.

00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:25.510
OK.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.250
So it sounds like you all
have lots of opinions,

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.190
which will be good.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.959
So to get started-- and
actually, I don't like the

00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:37.000
term lecturer because I don't
get to lecture very much--

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.570
I'm going to start by handing
around some index cards and I

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:45.970
want you to take a few minutes
and generate some examples of

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.850
ethical elements, procedures,
episodes, games, the more

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:52.890
specific you can
be the better.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.440
And I'm actually going to
collect these at the end

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.262
because, as Phil mentioned, I'm
teaching this ethics and

00:01:59.262 --> 00:02:01.166
games class and I want
to bring in more

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:02.118
examples of this stuff.

00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:04.498
So this will actually
be useful for me.

00:02:04.498 --> 00:02:06.770
AUDIENCE: Talking
about in games.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:07.580
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.740
So we were just talking about
Fable, but if you could think

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.810
of a more specific example from
it, a level, different

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.195
choices that you get with
characters in certain games,

00:02:19.195 --> 00:02:23.187
whatever you can come up with
in terms of are there

00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:26.680
interesting ethical dilemmas,
situations.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.678
AUDIENCE: Wait.

00:02:27.678 --> 00:02:31.750
Is this within the context of
the game, or are we talking

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.373
about in a multiplayer game
your conduct with other

00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:35.240
players, and this
kind of thing?

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.456
AUDIENCE: Or, when I was in
Owen's office whether or not

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:40.050
to steal the swag.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.210
GUEST SPEAKER: You can do it
within MMOs as well, or a

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.660
multiplayer game.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.783
You can continue to write
examples [UNINTELLIGIBLE] if

00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:50.226
you can think of them.

00:02:50.226 --> 00:02:52.150
But just to get you started--

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.400
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:02:57.441 --> 00:02:58.720
GUEST SPEAKER: Well then
you can start by

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.412
giving me an example.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.755
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:03:00.755 --> 00:03:01.160
OK.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.800
So I have a couple.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.210
Fighting games, obviously,
there's always kind of

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.800
questions and debate about
whether something's cheap or

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.680
viable or should you be
allowed to do it.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.792
Yeah, so if maybe--

00:03:15.792 --> 00:03:18.072
I'm trying to think how to
describe it-- so, people who

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:21.310
play casually will tend to say,
oh, if you do this with

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.860
this character then it's cheap
and you shouldn't do that,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.958
whereas the community at large
and people who play seriously

00:03:27.958 --> 00:03:30.946
tend to say, well, if you can
do something you should be

00:03:30.946 --> 00:03:34.183
allowed to do it, and if
you can't overcome it

00:03:34.183 --> 00:03:36.080
that's your own fault.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.000
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.390
So violating a community norm?

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.550
AUDIENCE: Yeah
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.914
GUEST SPEAKER: Even if it's
allowed in the rules but it's

00:03:43.914 --> 00:03:45.882
against a community norm.

00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:47.132
OK.

00:03:53.262 --> 00:03:57.230
It's like using
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.720
You hadn't thought about that.

00:04:16.154 --> 00:04:16.652
Evan?

00:04:16.652 --> 00:04:17.648
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:17.648 --> 00:04:19.142
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, sorry.

00:04:19.142 --> 00:04:21.134
AUDIENCE: I have a bunch of
stuff, which is related to

00:04:21.134 --> 00:04:24.640
griefing just generally.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.180
So let's just go for the one
which is one of my favorites.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.255
So this is griefing for comic
effect, or role playing

00:04:34.255 --> 00:04:38.390
griefing but doing it in a way
which you are then going to

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:39.350
write a blog about.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.510
And from your character's
perspective the

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.010
griefing makes sense.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:47.770
So there is a particular player
that played EverQuest.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:50.860
His name is Fansy
the Famous Bard.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:58.150
He was on the good team's side
and he griefed evil because

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.210
evil is bad.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.900
So people who had played evil
characters, he would run

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.730
around and annoy in a really
frustrating way, which

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:09.048
eventually caused the makers
of EverQuest to change the

00:05:09.048 --> 00:05:12.025
rules of the server so that
people could kill him, where

00:05:12.025 --> 00:05:13.755
previously they weren't
allowed to.

00:05:13.755 --> 00:05:15.930
GUEST SPEAKER: What did he do?

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.790
AUDIENCE: There was this
mechanic in EverQuest where if

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.834
you were close to a monster
it became aggressive

00:05:22.834 --> 00:05:24.100
and attacked you.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.220
So he had a low level character
who was able to run

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.280
really fast and--

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.000
GUEST SPEAKER: I have
heard of him.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.720
AUDIENCE: --he would run around
and then run over the

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.976
top of evil people, and then the
monsters would detach from

00:05:34.976 --> 00:05:38.554
him and then kill them.

00:05:38.554 --> 00:05:44.360
Oh yeah, and the catch
was that the server

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.260
was a mobile server.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.250
Aside from-- that is why-- so
you can't complain to GMs

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.970
about a player's behavior,
aside from there was a

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.890
built-in protection for players
so that if they're

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:01.460
level four or below-- so a very
low level-- they can't be

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.670
killed by higher
level players.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.650
And so he just sat on
the cusp of that.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:08.398
Yet there's still a way to use
his ability to go around and

00:06:08.398 --> 00:06:09.780
grief people from
a higher level.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.000
It was pretty great.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.600
And he did it all totally in
character as well, which is

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.254
what makes this a good grief.

00:06:21.611 --> 00:06:23.559
AUDIENCE: Is that me?

00:06:23.559 --> 00:06:24.809
OK.

00:06:26.968 --> 00:06:31.838
I think of Portal when they ask
you to use your portable

00:06:31.838 --> 00:06:32.325
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:34.145
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:06:34.145 --> 00:06:35.500
You get to burn up the Cube.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.150
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.955
You don't want to, and
they make you.

00:06:37.955 --> 00:06:41.507
And you've tried to leave
without it a few times and you

00:06:41.507 --> 00:06:43.735
try to break the machine
and it doesn't work.

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:45.220
It's very sad.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.695
GUEST SPEAKER: I feel bad.

00:06:47.695 --> 00:06:48.685
[INAUDIBLE]

00:06:48.685 --> 00:06:48.808
OK.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:49.180
How about you?

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.434
AUDIENCE: I thought of Jason
Rohrer's Sleep Is Death.

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:55.382
GUEST SPEAKER: What
is that like?

00:06:55.382 --> 00:06:57.750
AUDIENCE: It's a more of like a
story telling tool where you

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.120
have one player that can control
the 2D world and then

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.859
you have one player who is
the player, and they can

00:07:03.859 --> 00:07:05.104
interact with it.

00:07:05.104 --> 00:07:08.390
And each player has 30 seconds
to make some kind of action,

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.450
and then you make a story
going back and forth.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.020
And what happens is--

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.930
you can play online with people,
and the controller

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.800
character or player will have
some kind of story that they

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:20.250
want to tell.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:23.290
And so it's up to the player
whether or not they want to go

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.690
along with the story and act
like a character in that world

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.360
or try and break the story.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:29.300
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.240
What's the name of it again?

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:31.150
AUDIENCE: Sleep Is Death.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.470
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.935
I forgot what it's called.

00:07:37.935 --> 00:07:41.895
GUEST SPEAKER: Actually that's
more griefing, would you say?

00:07:41.895 --> 00:07:43.380
AUDIENCE: Kind of.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.330
AUDIENCE: Is that by the same
person as [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:48.825
Passage?

00:07:48.825 --> 00:07:51.300
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.400
AUDIENCE: In Ocarina of Time
there's the treasure box

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.110
gambling game.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.830
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] achieve if
you use the Lens of Truth.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:58.865
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:07:58.865 --> 00:08:04.696
So there's a built-in mechanic
that allows you to cheat.

00:08:04.696 --> 00:08:07.131
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
legit.

00:08:07.131 --> 00:08:10.060
AUDIENCE: Yeah [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:14.540
use it anyway so it's not
like a [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.410
AUDIENCE: I just like include
the [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:20.990
AUDIENCE: Lucky man.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:25.150
AUDIENCE: So in Civ IV for each
and every sector there's

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:29.130
some sort of slavery mechanic,
but I guess in Civ IV's

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.070
example I know.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:35.350
Many players don't seem to mind
the fact that what is

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.649
considered slavery is ethically
wrong, but in the

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:43.200
game it's affectionately called
whipping, and it's

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.480
considered a very efficient
way of converting food--

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:45.840
GUEST SPEAKER: What's
it called?

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:46.730
AUDIENCE: Whipping.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.710
AUDIENCE: Whipping.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:51.185
AUDIENCE: You whip and then
you [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:08:55.145 --> 00:08:56.135
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:59.245
So it's efficient but
it's slavery.

00:08:59.245 --> 00:09:02.330
AUDIENCE: You use granaries to
offset the population loss,

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.700
and if you [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:09.000
farms it becomes a very
effective way to produce,

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.440
especially in the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.757
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
a lot of people that way.

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:21.206
AUDIENCE: So in The
Sims [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:29.286 --> 00:09:31.405
GUEST SPEAKER: But you
have to watch them

00:09:31.405 --> 00:09:32.709
flail around and die.

00:09:32.709 --> 00:09:35.690
AUDIENCE: And they get
tired and [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:47.200
AUDIENCE: A more obscure example
that goes along with

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.440
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] griefing is
discussions over the point in

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.510
the game the community
is in MMOs.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:59.410
Specifically, there was
apparently a huge problem in

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:03.550
an obscure MMO called Skyress
where a large community of the

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.660
players was there to basically
anthropomorph and RP.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:09.110
A large community of the players
was there to play to

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:12.110
win because it had a large
combat economy system.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:16.820
And this, tactically, almost
tore the game apart because it

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:20.910
was such a small number of
people over what should be the

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.165
point to this game when,
mechanically, somebody thinks

00:10:23.165 --> 00:10:24.760
they're alive.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:30.870
Also in Canara Rey you can blow
up people, and they'll

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:32.569
even put the star.

00:10:32.569 --> 00:10:36.241
It was traumatizing.

00:10:36.241 --> 00:10:39.424
AUDIENCE: Yeah, but they're in
a giant ball of awesome.

00:10:39.424 --> 00:10:40.828
AUDIENCE: That's true.

00:10:40.828 --> 00:10:43.935
GUEST SPEAKER: Well, it's
not really murder but--

00:10:43.935 --> 00:10:46.080
AUDIENCE: I didn't want
to get starred.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.550
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.020
AUDIENCE: It's starification.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:55.790
AUDIENCE: If they don't
suffocate on the inside of the

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:56.750
kevlar first.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:58.670
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:00.542
GUEST SPEAKER: It's like the
Borg, once you're in there you

00:11:00.542 --> 00:11:02.510
know it's a better way
of [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:05.890
You're grateful.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:15.210
AUDIENCE: So in F.E.A.R there's
a nail gun, and when

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:19.600
you kill people with it the game
does a really good job of

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:23.970
spectacularly nailing them to
surfaces in the environment.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:27.460
So if someone jumps and you
shoot them in the shoulder you

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.110
can nail them up
to the ceiling.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.540
And if you nail them multiple
times you can have them

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:33.955
hanging by a hand, and by their
head, and by a foot from

00:11:33.955 --> 00:11:36.060
the ceiling or from the wall.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.310
Anyway, [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:45.160
on forums there were entire
threads devoted to sharing

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:51.310
screenshots of your most
spectacular nails, just the

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.150
absolutely, most gruesome--

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:57.490
five nails to the eye suspending
a guy from

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.443
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
kind of thing.

00:11:59.443 --> 00:12:00.886
And it's pretty--

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:07.370
Anyway, and there's a lot of
debate on these threads

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:08.960
because most people will be
like, yeah, look at this

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.410
picture this one's awesome and
everything it has blood

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:12.310
spatters everywhere
and all that.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:14.835
But every third or fourth post
it would be like, you guys are

00:12:14.835 --> 00:12:19.330
so messed up, this is wrong.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:24.650
And it's not real, you idiot,
but that fact that you like it

00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:25.790
is still real.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:29.020
And people would argue at
great lengths over this.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.460
And I remember chancing upon
these forums and being like,

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:33.481
is this messed up?

00:12:33.481 --> 00:12:34.320
I'm not sure.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.850
GUEST SPEAKER: So is it more
efficient in the game to use

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.530
the nail gun to kill
people than other

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:41.015
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:12:41.015 --> 00:12:42.770
AUDIENCE: It's a hell
of a lot more fun.

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:44.896
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:12:44.896 --> 00:12:47.343
But it's not like the game
forces you to do it if you

00:12:47.343 --> 00:12:48.460
would not want to.

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:49.910
AUDIENCE: No it doesn't.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.519
You could use other guns, but
at some point, if you don't

00:12:52.519 --> 00:12:54.065
have enough ammo for the other
