00:19:26.714 --> 00:19:29.615
Philip.

00:19:29.615 --> 00:19:33.203
AUDIENCE: In a LARP that I
played a long time ago I was

00:19:33.203 --> 00:19:36.500
allowed to draw one item from
a location every hour.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:40.280
The game ran from noon to
midnight on three different

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:42.470
days but I couldn't play
before 2:00 PM.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:44.676
So I come in at 2:00 PM.

00:19:44.676 --> 00:19:48.556
How many items was I allowed to
draw when I came into the

00:19:48.556 --> 00:19:50.630
game given that I already missed
two hours of the game?

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:55.190
And if the answer is, well, I
can make that up, then what

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.422
happens if I miss an hour
half way through the--

00:19:57.422 --> 00:20:01.330
and if I forget.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.820
No one's looking into the box.

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:04.950
GUEST SPEAKER: What
did you do?

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:05.923
AUDIENCE: What did I do?

00:20:05.923 --> 00:20:06.650
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:20:06.650 --> 00:20:07.376
AUDIENCE: What did I do?

00:20:07.376 --> 00:20:10.285
I think I drew more than one
item at the beginning of the

00:20:10.285 --> 00:20:14.100
game and I made sure
I never forgot.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:15.435
So, honor system.

00:20:15.435 --> 00:20:17.856
A lot of LARPs are based
on the honor system.

00:20:23.832 --> 00:20:27.990
AUDIENCE: So in Final Fantasy
VII, like a lot of Japanese

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.060
RPGs, you get used to the idea
of walking into people's

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.750
houses and just digging in the
items you find, except in FF

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.620
VII they actually mix it
up a couple of times.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:37.440
There are a couple of instances
where you go in

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:39.570
there and do that and maybe it's
fairly insignificant, you

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.800
could buy it easily with a
couple pieces of gold or gil

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:43.915
or whatever it is.

00:20:43.915 --> 00:20:45.940
And then the person walks out of
their kitchen or something,

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.630
and it's, did you just
take my item?

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:50.070
And you have the option
saying yes or no.

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:53.070
And if you say no then you get
to keep it but if you say yes

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.150
they're, what the hell?

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:57.482
Give it back.

00:20:57.482 --> 00:21:00.891
I thought it was interesting.

00:21:00.891 --> 00:21:02.717
AUDIENCE: I have something
that hasn't really been

00:21:02.717 --> 00:21:05.790
touched on.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:09.721
When you're running a very large
scale Guild in an MMO

00:21:09.721 --> 00:21:12.777
you reach a point where you need
to distribute from these

00:21:12.777 --> 00:21:20.320
high end The community optimal
thing to do is for the people

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.565
who are in charge of the Guild
to allocate who gets what so

00:21:23.565 --> 00:21:26.305
that you can, for instance, kick
out the characters that's

00:21:26.305 --> 00:21:27.910
most important for the Guild
to have for the [? game ?]

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:29.760
to progress.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:33.040
But for the most part, actual
members of the Guild don't

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:36.380
really like that because you
would like to receive some

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.235
kind of reward for having put
in time to the Guild.

00:21:39.235 --> 00:21:42.445
And going a long time without
ever having gotten anything is

00:21:42.445 --> 00:21:46.130
really frustrating, so people
come up with these intricate

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:52.920
systems for DKP for distributing
your resources,

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:56.564
essentially, to try to balance
the interests of the

00:21:56.564 --> 00:21:58.387
individual and the interest
of the community.

00:21:58.387 --> 00:22:02.110
GUEST SPEAKER: How many of you
are unsure what DKP is?

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:04.576
Dragon Kill Points.

00:22:04.576 --> 00:22:08.110
There are these sometimes
elaborate schemes for if you

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:13.190
participate in these raids,
you get so many points.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:18.050
And the idea is the Guild claims
everything that drops

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:18.980
physically.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.555
And you build up points, and at
some point you have enough

00:22:22.555 --> 00:22:25.020
to buy a certain item.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.440
So let's say you're on a raid
and you just got something

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:28.860
really cool last time.

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:31.130
If something drops this time
that you could really use,

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:33.580
even though you're there you
don't necessarily get it

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.920
because you just spent
all your DKP.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:40.670
And there's lots of Guild drama
over how these DKPs--

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:42.926
AUDIENCE: Primary source
of Guild drama.

00:22:42.926 --> 00:22:44.335
AUDIENCE: Have you
been playing?

00:22:44.335 --> 00:22:45.185
GUEST SPEAKER: Hm?

00:22:45.185 --> 00:22:48.240
AUDIENCE: Dan, have you
been playing this?

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.026
AUDIENCE: You end up by
raids and then you

00:22:50.026 --> 00:22:51.840
catch them before breakfast.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:54.616
GUEST SPEAKER: And there
are black out dates.

00:22:54.616 --> 00:22:56.380
AUDIENCE: Running one of these
things is actually

00:22:56.380 --> 00:23:00.120
surprisingly complicated and
hard, particularly--

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.880
so my little experience,
I ran a Guild which

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:06.100
incorporated this.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.710
One problem that happens is if
you have fixed prices for

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:13.370
things then you get DKP
inflation as prices change.

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:15.990
And what happens is-- and it's
incredibly complicated-- to

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.640
where, now, you have to reprice
everything constantly.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:20.180
So instead we went for an
auction-based system where

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.840
people bid on things.

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:25.470
And then prices correct
themselves.

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:29.360
However, people can collude in
auctions, which then causes a

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.775
resentment in people who aren't
in on the collusion,

00:23:31.775 --> 00:23:34.970
and the whole thing is
just a nightmare.

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.890
And now I don't run a Guild
anymore and I'm really happy.

00:23:41.183 --> 00:23:44.610
AUDIENCE: In games like Pokemon
or Animal Crossing you

